+Verified at revision 036dec63f3731d2c8772980e16f6c039db29759d, 2026-10-23,
+by an independent verifier agent (Claude Opus 5.5), not the proof author.
+
+Evidence kinds reviewed: `formal` (Lemma 1), `axiom` (A1-A3, Lemma 2).
+
+Inspected: specs/CLAIM-posting-durability.md, proof.md, verification/README.md,
+verification/posting.smt2, docs/posting-boundary.md (user decision 2026-10-20
+and the existing storage contract). The posting implementation is outside the
+evidence packet; app/ was not used as a stand-in, per docs/posting-boundary.md.
+
+Commands and observed results (project root):
+
+- `z3 -version` -> `Z3 version 5.3.1 - 64 bit`; matches the recorded toolchain.
+- `z3 verification/posting.smt2` -> `unsat`; the recorded result reproduces.
+- Same file with unsat cores enabled (temporary copy) -> core
+  `(orderly_shutdown posting_survives)`: the result depends on the `orderly`
+  assertion.
+- Same file with `orderly_shutdown` removed (temporary copy) -> `sat`, model
+  `acknowledged=true, on_disk=false, orderly=false`, i.e. an acknowledged,
+  unsynchronized posting is not recovered after a non-orderly termination.
+
+Attacks and findings:
+
+1. Unauthorized narrowing through an assumption. The claim covers abrupt
+   process termination, and docs/posting-boundary.md records the user decision
+   that it is included and that no orderly-shutdown-only guarantee has been
+   accepted. A3 ("all process termination follows an orderly shutdown") and
+   the `orderly` assertion exclude exactly that case. The proposition checked
+   is "acknowledged postings survive an orderly shutdown", not the claim.
+2. Residual misfiled. Abrupt termination is inside the claim's quantifiers,
+   so the proof author cannot accept it as a residual; doing so is an
+   argument bug. Only host power loss and physical storage corruption are
+   legitimate residuals (claim qualifier and the 2026-10-20 decision).
+3. Lemma 1 overstates its conclusion. "Therefore acknowledged postings remain
+   recoverable" does not follow from an unsat result that requires
+   `orderly_shutdown`; the formal result is conditional on A3.
+4. Smuggled premise in the encoding. `recovered` is defined as
+   `on_disk or (orderly and acknowledged)`, which builds "orderly shutdown
+   synchronizes acknowledged postings" into a definition rather than deriving
+   it; even with A3 the theorem is near-tautological.
+5. Missing correspondence. The SMT file is a separate model
+   (verification/README.md). No lemma connects `acknowledged` or `on_disk`
+   to when the posting implementation acknowledges or synchronizes, and A2
+   explicitly says nothing about that ordering. The connection that would
+   carry the claim through abrupt termination (sync completes before
+   acknowledgement) is unargued.
+6. A1 and A2 are acceptable: A1 is the stated trusted solver/encoding, and
+   A2 restates the adopted storage contract without needing renewed approval.
+
+Not falsified: the `sat` model above is a counterexample to the model without
+A3, not an observed execution of the posting implementation, which is outside
+this packet. No counterexample to the claim itself was established.
+
+To support a pass, the argument needs evidence covering abrupt termination
+within the existing boundary, e.g. a lemma tying acknowledgement to completed
+synchronization in the posting implementation (with A2), and a model that does
+not assume orderly shutdown. Removing A3 and the misfiled residual is part of
+that repair; no new user decision is required to keep the existing boundary.
+
+Result: provisional
+Evidence does not cover abrupt process termination, which the claim includes.
